.TH GIT-CLEANUP-REPO 1 "2026-10-08" "git-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-cleanup-repo \- delete safely merged local branches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cleanup-repo
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git cleanup-repo
resolves the remote fetch endpoint, fetches the exact base (the remote's default
branch unless
.B \-\-base
names another) without updating remote-tracking refs or FETCH_HEAD, then
deletes stale local branches whose exact object IDs are proven merged.
.PP
By default it deletes local branches that are already merged into the base
branch. This includes squash-, rebase-, and cherry-pick-merged branches: patch
IDs locate candidates, but exact raw-delta evidence is also required. A squash
candidate must be a transition after the branch merge base on the pinned base's
first-parent history. Its first-parent-to-commit delta must exactly match the
branch's net delta; this includes transitions introduced by merge commits.
Later mainline edits do not erase that historical proof; byte, mode, path,
deletion, or gitlink differences still reject it. Multi-commit rebase and
cherry-pick
matches retain an exact-current-snapshot check. A branch whose complete tree
equals a snapshot on the base's first-parent history also counts as merged,
which covers stacked landings that neither detector matches. With
.B gh
and
.B jq
and a GitHub remote (github.com, or another host gh has a credential for; an SSH
Host alias remote such as work:owner/repo resolves through
.BR "ssh \-G" ),
a branch whose tip belongs to a merged pull request whose
merge commit is in the pinned base counts too; an open pull request containing
the tip or named after the branch keeps it. Branch and upstream-state
enumeration must complete and validate before the first deletion. Each
candidate is rechecked, and its ref is deleted only if it still has the exact
proven object ID. It can also select branches whose own-name remote branch is
gone or every local branch except the base branch. The command does not
execute configured fetch or prune mappings; other remote-tracking state must be
refreshed separately when needed.
.PP
Base selection does not depend on configured fetch refspecs or short ref names.
The command fetches exactly
.BR refs/heads/ base
without tags, validates and pins its native SHA-1 or SHA-256 object ID, and
checks the local fast-forward relationship before changing anything. It then
creates or fast-forwards the base using that same object ID. A divergent base
fails before the current branch or local base is changed. A worktree that has
the base checked out is fast-forwarded in place with
.BR "git merge \-\-ff\-only" ;
another worktree that has local changes or an active operation, cannot be
inspected, or refuses the fast-forward is left alone, and merges are then
proven against the unchanged local base. A main worktree whose files live
outside its Git directory, such as a
.B core.worktree
checkout or a bare repository with an index used through an external work
tree, is never updated from another checkout; cleanup reports that it is not
updating the base there and leaves that checkout to its own tooling. The same
holds when cleanup runs in a main checkout that Git reaches only through
.BR GIT_DIR / GIT_WORK_TREE
or
.BR core.worktree :
it is never switched or merged into, and is planned like a linked worktree;
branches already merged into its local base are still deleted. Otherwise
the main worktree switches to the base. A linked worktree never switches to
the base; it advances only the base ref with an expected-old-value
.BR "git update\-ref" ,
by fast-forward only, and leaves the base alone when a rebase or bisect
reserves it or worktrees cannot be inspected; without a local base, it stops
instead. From the main worktree,
.B git switch
itself refuses a base that a rebase or bisect reserves. Updates to a checkout
other than the current one are always reported.
The branch checked out in the current checkout is never deleted. When the base
update is skipped, the command still exits 0, but its final summary line ends
with
.RI "" base " not updated (" reason )
so the base is not mistaken for current. A dry run stops with exit status 2
and
.B would refuse:
and the reason wherever the real run refuses before changing anything,
including Git refusing the base switch or fast-forward because an untracked or
ignored file is in the way, which it predicts by checking for untracked or
ignored files at the paths the base would add.
Dry-run
uses isolated temporary object storage.
.PP
The command retains the single raw configured fetch URL for both remote
inspection and fetch, allowing Git to apply any
.B insteadOf
rewrite exactly once per operation. Remotes with zero or multiple fetch URLs
are rejected. Git 2.29 or newer is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fIbranch\fR
Base branch to keep and update.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remote " " \fIname\fR
Remote fetch endpoint to resolve and fetch from. Defaults to
.BR origin .
.TP
.B \-\-gone
Also select branches whose own remote branch (the same name on a real remote)
is gone. A branch tracking a different branch, such as its base, is never
selected this way, because that upstream's deletion proves nothing about the
branch's own commits. Neither is one whose upstream was a default branch
.RB ( main ,
.BR master ,
.BR trunk ,
or the branch the remote's recorded HEAD names): a default branch disappears
when it is renamed, not after landing. A gone upstream says the remote branch
was retired, not that every local commit reached it, so with pull request
evidence a branch whose tip GitHub has never seen
.RB ( unpublished )
or whose lookup failed
.RB ( pr\-unknown )
is kept too. Without that evidence such a branch is still selected, and its
commits made after the remote branch went are lost.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Delete all local branches except the base branch.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-worktrees
Remove eligible, operation-free linked worktrees for branches selected for
deletion. The current worktree and the main worktree are never removed, so a
branch still checked out in either after the base update is kept. Removal never
uses
.BR \-\-force ,
and Git's final clean check sees untracked files even in a repository that sets
.BR status.showUntrackedFiles=no .
.TP
.BI \-\-worktree " path"
Remove only this linked worktree; repeat for more. Implies
.BR \-\-remove\-worktrees .
A branch checked out in an unlisted worktree is kept. A listed worktree whose
branch cleanup keeps is still retired, keeping the branch, when its HEAD is
detached and proven merged, its own-name upstream (not a default branch) is
gone, or, with
.BR \-\-include\-closed ,
only a closed unmerged pull request contains it. Given together with an
explicit
.BR \-\-remove\-worktrees ,
every eligible worktree is removed, as with
.B \-\-remove\-worktrees
alone.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-update\-base
Prove merges against the pinned remote base without creating, switching to, or
fast-forwarding the local base. The current branch is still kept, and a dirty
or mid-operation current checkout is allowed because nothing touches it.
When the base was inferred, from a stale
.IB remote /HEAD
or the main, master, or trunk fallback, and a fetching run finds the remote no
longer has it, the remote's own default branch is used instead, with a note,
and the old local default stays protected like the base. Without this option
that case stops and suggests
.B \-\-base
with the remote's default. An explicit
.B \-\-base
is never redirected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fetch
Prove against the existing
.IB remote / base
remote-tracking ref as of the last fetch instead of fetching. A ref that is
merely behind keeps more branches, but if the remote base was rewound since,
the ref can still contain commits the remote dropped. Pull request evidence,
which needs the network, is not used.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print one tab-separated record per decision on standard output, as described
under
.BR PORCELAIN ,
and only diagnostics on standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-age " days"
Keep a branch proven only by ancestry that was created or moved less than this
many days ago; such a branch looks exactly like a fast-forward-merged one. Its
reflog says when. Reflog entries expire, leaving an empty reflog, so a branch
whose reflog is empty is dated by its tip's commit, which is safe because
creating or moving a logged branch writes an entry. A branch with no reflog at
all was never logged (with
.BR core.logAllRefUpdates=false ,
or created by
.B git worktree add \-b
in a bare repository); its age is unknown and it is kept. At most 99999.
.TP
.BI \-\-retire\-worktree " path"
Remove this linked worktree; repeat for more. The caller vouches for the
removal and every removal gate still applies. Its branch is kept unless cleanup
proves it merged; a branch kept as
.B open\-pr
or
.B too\-new
keeps the worktree too, and so does a detached HEAD that no branch, remote
branch, or tag reaches
.RB ( unreachable\-head ),
and a branch whose pull request lookup failed
.RB ( pr\-unknown ).
A gate code such as
.B unique\-commits
keeps it like any other worktree.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-closed
Also retire a selected worktree whose branch only a closed, unmerged pull
request contains. The branch is kept.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-interface\-version
Print the porcelain interface version, an integer that grows whenever the
options, records, codes, or exit statuses gain or change anything a caller
relies on, and exit. Version 1 added exit status 3, the
.BR pr\-unknown ,
.BR closed\-pr ,
.BR symref ,
and
.B submodule
codes, the
.B default\-upstream\-gone
detail,
.B pr\-unknown
keeping a requested retirement, and following a renamed remote default
branch. Version 2 added the
.B unpublished
and
.B unique\-commits
codes,
.B pr\-unknown
and
.B unpublished
keeping a branch from
.B \-\-gone
and its worktree from upstream-gone retirement,
.B \-\-min\-age
dating a branch whose reflog expired by its tip's commit (so
.B too\-new
can carry a commit time), and
.B submodule
also covering files in an unpopulated submodule directory.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH PORCELAIN
Each record is
.IR event ,
.IR subject ,
.IR code ,
and
.IR detail ,
separated by tabs. Backslash, tab, and newline inside a field are written as
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \et ,
and
.BR \en .
.TP
.BR delete\-branch ", " would\-delete\-branch
The subject is a branch, the code its proof
.RB ( merged ,
.BR content\-merged ,
.BR tree\-landed ,
.BR merged\-pr ,
.BR upstream\-gone ,
or
.BR all ),
and the detail its proven object ID.
.TP
.B keep\-branch
The subject is a branch and the code why it stays:
.B merge\-unproven
(detail
.BR upstream\-gone ,
.B default\-upstream\-gone
(its own upstream was a default branch, which
.B \-\-gone
never selects),
.BR other\-upstream\-gone ,
.BR upstream ,
or
.BR no\-upstream ),
.B open\-pr
(detail the pull request number),
.B closed\-pr
(no proof, and a pull request containing the tip was closed unmerged; detail
its number),
.B pr\-unknown
(no proof, and its pull requests could not be looked up;
.B \-\-gone
keeps it too),
.B unpublished
(no proof, and its own upstream is gone, but GitHub has never seen its tip, so
it holds commits that were never pushed;
.B \-\-gone
keeps it),
.B symref
(a symbolic ref under
.B refs/heads
points at it; detail the aliases' branch names, space-separated),
.B too\-new
(detail the reflog time, the tip's commit time once the reflog expired, or
empty when the age is unknown),
.BR current\-worktree ,
.BR submodule ,
.BR checked\-out ,
.BR main\-worktree ,
.BR locked ,
.BR dirty ,
.BR hidden ,
.BR prune\-failed ,
or
.B remove\-failed
(detail the worktree path),
.B operation
(detail the operation),
.B in\-use
(detail the process ID),
.B unique\-commits
(detail a commit that the worktree's own refs or HEAD reflog hold, that no
branch, tag, or remote branch reaches, and that no exception excuses; see
.BR SAFETY ),
.BR uninspectable ,
.BR branch\-changed ,
.BR reserved ,
.B checkout\-in\-flight
(detail the held lock),
or
.B ref\-delete\-failed
(detail the proven object ID).
.TP
.B keep\-worktree
The subject is a selected worktree and the code why it stays: the code that
kept its branch,
.BR current\-worktree ,
.BR main\-worktree ,
.BR missing ,
.BR not\-linked ,
.B uninspectable
(Git or the process view could not be read), the gate code that kept the
checkout
.RB ( locked ,
.BR dirty ,
.BR hidden ,
.BR operation ,
.BR submodule ,
.BR in\-use ,
.BR unique\-commits ,
.BR checkout\-in\-flight ,
.BR branch\-changed ,
.BR prune\-failed ,
or
.BR remove\-failed ),
or
.B unreachable\-head
(detail the detached HEAD).
.BR merge\-unproven ,
.BR closed\-pr ,
.BR pr\-unknown ,
.BR unpublished ,
and
.B symref
carry the branch as detail;
.B pr\-unknown
also keeps a requested retirement that could not confirm that no open pull
request holds the branch. Every selected worktree gets exactly one
.BR remove\-worktree ,
.BR would\-remove\-worktree ,
or
.B keep\-worktree
record.
.TP
.BR remove\-worktree ", " would\-remove\-worktree
The subject is a worktree path, the code why it goes (its branch's deletion
code, or a retirement code:
.BR requested ,
.BR upstream\-gone ,
.BR closed\-pr ,
or a detached HEAD's merge proof), and the detail its branch.
.TP
.BR prune\-entry ", " would\-prune\-entry
The subject is a worktree path and the detail the disposable entry.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Cleanup ran. Every decision was reported, including kept branches and failed
removals.
.TP
.B 2
Usage, repository, or remote error. Porcelain records already printed may be
incomplete.
.TP
.B 3
The remote base could not be reached or fetched. Nothing was decided, so
retrying with
.B \-\-no\-fetch
is safe.
.SH WORKTREE PRUNING
Before removing an otherwise eligible linked worktree, the command recognizes
two explicit classes of disposable top-level entries. A real top-level
directory with no tracked content is disposable when its real, untracked
.B CACHEDIR.TAG
file begins with the standard
.B Signature: 8a477f597d28d172789f06886806bc55
line; a directory holding tracked files is a source directory and never
counts. A repository can authorize another literal top-level entry with a
repository-local, multi-valued setting:
.PP
.nf
git config --local --add cleanupRepo.worktreePrunePath .cache
.fi
.PP
This setting grants deletion authority. Values containing a path separator,
traversal, a newline, or the Git administrative name
.B .git
are rejected. Global and command-line configuration cannot grant this
authority. The command prunes an authorized entry with a path-limited
.B git clean -fdx
and repeats all worktree checks afterward. Unknown local content present at
preflight prevents every prune. Dry-run only reports entries that would be
pruned.
.SH SAFETY
Unless
.B \-\-no\-update\-base
is given, the command refuses to run with a dirty current worktree or active
rebase, merge, cherry-pick, or revert state. Except for explicitly disposable
entries,
a linked worktree is preserved when it has uncommitted, untracked, ignored, or
index-hidden local content, is unreadable, is locked with
.BR "git worktree lock" ,
has an active rebase, merge, cherry-pick, revert, or bisect, contains a
populated submodule (which Git cannot remove) or files in an unpopulated
submodule's directory (which status does not show), is the working
directory of a visible process (read from
.B /proc
or
.BR lsof ;
worktrees are kept when neither can list working directories; open files held
from another directory are not detected), or cannot be removed without force.
It is also preserved when its HEAD reflog or its own refs
.RB ( refs/worktree ,
.BR refs/bisect ,
.BR refs/rewritten ),
which go with it, hold a commit that no branch, tag, or remote branch reaches
.RB ( unique\-commits ).
A commit those refs hold is protected unless a branch, tag, remote branch, or
the stash reaches it. So is every commit the HEAD
reflog names, on either side of any entry (a checkout, reset, amend, rebase, or
a tool's own GIT_REFLOG_ACTION), on a branch or detached; old sides are read
from the reflog file itself, since after gc expires old entries a surviving
entry's old side can be the only record of a commit. With reftable reflogs
there is no such file, so a worktree whose HEAD reflog has entries is kept
.RB ( uninspectable ).
These do not count: commits a branch, tag, remote branch, or the stash
reaches; commits named by the reflog of a branch that still exists or that the
same run deletes (an amended, reset, or rebased branch tip), which deleting
that branch drops; the current HEAD's history, which the removal reason
covers; the tips of branches the same run deletes; commits whose work is
proven in the base by the same proofs that delete branches, and the commits
they were built on (proofs run after the cheaper checks; once eight have
failed, the rest stay protected); commits replaced rather than discarded,
where an amend or a rebase step that makes a new commit (pick, reword, edit,
fixup, squash, merge, or continue) moved HEAD to a commit that is safe; and
clean copies an aborted rebase made (pick, reword, edit, fixup, squash), whose
originals are still on the branch or the tip it returned to; a copy excuses
only itself. A reset, a
rebase's reset or abort, or a checkout discards or leaves, so detached work
reset away (even to a newer origin/main), a conflict resolved before an abort,
and commits made or amended by hand during an aborted rebase keep the worktree.
A worktree whose own index or HEAD lock is held is kept, and while any
worktree holds one every branch is kept
.RB ( checkout\-in\-flight ),
because a
.B git switch
in progress holds those locks after resolving the branch it checks out; a lock
left by a crashed Git keeps branches until it is removed.
Updating the base never overwrites an ignored file with a newly tracked one:
in the invoking checkout the command stops with status 2, back where it
started (the message says so if switching back failed), and a base checked
out in another worktree is left not updated. In a sparse checkout Git would
overwrite such a file with only a warning, so the command checks first there
and holds back, possibly also for a path outside the sparse patterns that Git
would have left alone. It also
refuses to switch away from a branch with no commits yet.
Symbolic refs under
.B refs/heads
(aliases such as master pointing at main) and the branches they point at are
never deleted.
Git cannot atomically combine worktree
reservation checks with ref deletion, so callers must not create, switch, or
mutate worktrees concurrently with cleanup. Observable state is rechecked, and
expected-old-object-ID ref deletion preserves a branch that advances during
cleanup.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-pr-land (1),
.BR git-branch-audit (1),
.BR git-worktree-audit (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
